```{r example-curves, fig.width = 9, fig.height = 5, out.width = "100%", fig.cap = "**Figure 2.** Fitted curves from all eight approaches applied to a single simulated dataset from scenario 8, with the true curve in black. Each panel shows the data that approach was given: intact under A and B2, floored to zero under B1, B3, E and F, the censoring bound under C, and truncated above 12.2 under D. Open symbols mark the nine rows the convention altered. Curves are drawn from posterior mean parameters, which for a non-linear model is not the same as the posterior mean curve; the difference is largest for B2. The vertical line is the true ErC50."}
xx <- seq(0, 18.5, length.out = 400)
ex_curves <- do.call(rbind, lapply(seq_len(nrow(example_pars)), function(i) {
  p <- example_pars[i, ]
  data.frame(arm = p$arm, x = xx,
             y = nec4param_curve(xx, p$top, p$bot, p$beta, p$nec))
}))
ex_curves$arm <- factor(ex_curves$arm, levels = arm_levels)
true_curve <- data.frame(
  x = xx, y = nec4param_curve(xx, example_truth$top, example_truth$bot,
                              example_truth$beta, example_truth$nec))


# Each panel shows the data its own fit was given, not one intact copy repeated
# eight times: B1, B3, E and F were handed floored values, C the bound with the
# row declared censored, and D a series truncated at the concentration where
# approach A's own posterior mean first reaches zero -- which is the rule the
# approach is defined by, recovered here from A's stored parameters rather than
# hard-coded. Only A and B2 saw the measurements as simulated. Open symbols mark
# the rows the convention altered.
ex_x <- sort(unique(ex_dat$x[ex_dat$x > 0]))
ex_A <- example_pars[example_pars$arm == "A", ]
ex_truncation <- min(ex_x[nec4param_curve(ex_x, ex_A$top, ex_A$bot,
                                          ex_A$beta, ex_A$nec) <= 0])

ex_points <- do.call(rbind, lapply(arm_levels, function(a) {
  d <- subset(ex_dat, x > 0)
  d$altered <- FALSE
  if (a %in% c("B1", "B3", "E", "F", "C")) {
    d$altered <- d$y < 0
    d$y <- pmax(d$y, 0)
  } else if (a == "D") {
    d <- d[d$x < ex_truncation, ]
  }
  data.frame(arm = a, d)
}))
ex_points$arm <- factor(ex_points$arm, levels = arm_levels)

ggplot(subset(ex_curves, x > 0), aes(x = x, y = y)) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0, linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey50") +
  geom_vline(xintercept = example_truth$ErC50, linetype = "dotted",
             colour = "grey40") +
  geom_point(data = ex_points, aes(shape = altered), alpha = 0.5, size = 1) +
  geom_line(data = subset(true_curve, x > 0), linewidth = 1.1, colour = "grey15") +
  geom_line(aes(colour = arm), linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_shape_manual(values = c("FALSE" = 16, "TRUE" = 1), guide = "none") +
  scale_colour_manual(values = arm_cols, guide = "none") +
  facet_wrap(~ arm, ncol = 4) +
  scale_x_log10() +
  labs(x = "Concentration (log scale)",
       y = expression("Specific growth rate ("*d^-1*")")) +
  theme_classic(base_size = 9) +
  theme(strip.background = element_blank())
```

Arm A, the intact analysis, is the only one whose fitted curve follows the true
curve below zero. It recovers a lower asymptote of -0.430 against a true -0.476
and returns an ErC50 of 5.05 against a true 5.05.

Arms C and D, which withhold the magnitude of the negative values rather than
replacing them, both flatten out well above the true asymptote, at -0.036 and
-0.084 respectively. This is expected and is not the same failure as flooring:
neither approach has the information needed to place the asymptote, so the flat
region below zero is the prior's rather than the data's. What matters is that
their curves still track the true curve through the region where ErC10 and
ErC50 are read, which is above zero.

Arms B1, B2 and B3 flatten at exactly zero, and to do so they must make the
descent shallower and push `nec` to the right, which deforms the curve in the
region the toxicity estimates *are* read from. `nec` changes from 1.93 under
arm A to 2.18 under B1, 2.24 under B3 and 2.94 under B2, against a true value
of 1.3. The constraint applies below zero, but its consequences are felt above
it.

Arms E and F do the same thing through the family, and the displacement is
largest under F. E is barely distinguishable from B3 — `nec` 2.16 against B3's
2.24 — which is what it was constructed to be. F places `nec` at 5.05, nearly
four times the true value, and returns an ErC50 of 6.13 against a true 5.05.
That figure runs the *opposite* way to F's average across the 500 datasets of
this scenario, which is -5.6%; F has the largest RMSE of any approach, so a
single dataset cannot be used to characterise it. What the single dataset shows,
and what the aggregate confirms, is where the error enters: the position of
`nec`.

The fitted residual standard deviation shows the two mechanisms directly, and
they act in opposite directions. Replacing the negative values with zero
removes the most extreme low observations, which compresses the residual
variance at high concentration. Fixing the asymptote at zero leaves those
observations in place but puts them beyond the reach of the fitted curve, which
inflates it.

```{r, echo = FALSE}
# Restricted to the six Gaussian approaches on purpose: the column is a residual
# standard deviation, and E and F do not have one. Their dispersion parameters
# are reported in the text instead, on their own terms.
knitr::kable(
  example_pars |>
    filter(disp_par == "sigma") |>
    select(arm, bot, nec, dispersion),
  digits = c(0, 4, 2, 4),
  col.names = c("Approach", "fitted bot", "fitted nec", "fitted sigma"),
  caption = "**Table 2.** Posterior mean parameters from the six Gaussian fits to one simulated dataset. True values are bot = -0.476 and nec = 1.3."
)
```

Flooring the data (B1, B3) compresses the residual standard deviation to a
little over half that of arm A; fixing the asymptote (B2) roughly doubles it.
Arms E and F have no `sigma` to put in that table. E's Beta precision of 10.0
corresponds to a residual standard deviation of about 0.016 at the control once
the scaling is undone, close to the 0.012 of the floored Gaussian fits. F's
Gamma shape of 1.18 corresponds to a coefficient of variation of 92% at every
point on the curve, against a true control value of 9.6%: a Gamma with an
identity link fixes the coefficient of variation, so no single value fits both
the control scatter and the block of identical near-zero values that flooring
creates.

This is one dataset, and the individual differences between estimates are
within Monte Carlo noise. The aggregate results below, each averaging across 500
simulated datasets, are what the conclusions rest on.

```{r, echo = FALSE}
sim_results <- read.csv(text = "
arm,estimate,regime,rel_bias,coverage,rmse
A,ErC10,reaches,0.2409,0.8380,0.6399
