## 5.4 Effect of the concentration design

Before we compare the approaches we have to separate the scenarios into two
groups, because the amount of information about the lower asymptote differs
between them. Where the concentration series stops at or before the zero
crossing, no true mean is negative or only the highest concentration is, so
there is essentially nothing in the data that determines the asymptote. Where
the series runs past the crossing, the asymptote is identified.

```{r, echo = FALSE}
knitr::kable(
  sim_results |>
    filter(estimate == "ErC50") |>
    select(arm, regime, rel_bias, coverage) |>
    pivot_wider(names_from = regime, values_from = c(rel_bias, coverage)),
  digits = 3,
  col.names = c("Approach", "bias (reaches)", "bias (stops short)",
                "coverage (reaches)", "coverage (stops short)"),
  caption = "**Table 3.** Relative bias and 95% interval coverage for ErC50, averaged across the three `delta` levels at `R` = 2.3. *Reaches* means the concentration series extends past the concentration at which the true curve crosses zero growth, so about one observation in seven has a negative true mean and every convention has values to act on. *Stops short* means it ends at or before that crossing, so no true mean is negative or only the highest concentration is."
)
```

In the *stops short* column of Table 3 six of the eight approaches are within
about four percentage points of each other and B1 has the smallest bias of any.
That is not evidence that flooring reduces bias. Arm A is itself biased there,
by about -4%, from an asymptote the design never measured, and flooring happens
to offset that bias. Averaging the two regimes together would combine a real
effect with an artefact.

**All results below are from the scenarios that reach past the zero crossing.**
If essentially none of your observations are at or below zero growth, none of
this applies to your data.

## 5.5 ErC10 and ErC50

Within the reaching regime the ordering is consistent. Arms A and C are
unbiased to within one per cent for ErC50 and arm D is close behind. The three
approaches that impose the zero boundary deliberately are biased low by 5 to 10
per cent — B1 -4.9%, B3 -6.5%, B2 -9.5% — in the direction of making the
substance appear more toxic than it is. Of the two family-imposed approaches, E
is -8.7% and F is -1.6%.

F's -1.6% must not be read as accuracy. It is a mean over three `delta` levels
of a quantity that changes sign across them, and its RMSE is 1.11, nearly three
times arm A's 0.38 and the largest of any approach.

The bias deepens as the true lower asymptote gets further below zero. Across
`delta` = 2, 4 and 8, B1 changes from -2.8% to -5.6% to -6.3%, B3 from -4.3% to
-7.6% to -7.6%, B2 from -8.5% to -10.2% to -9.9%, and E from -5.6% to -10.6% to
-9.9%, while A and C stay inside ±3.4% with no trend. The further the data
descend below the boundary, the more the boundary distorts. F has the steepest
gradient and is the only approach whose bias changes sign across the three
levels: +14.0%, then -5.6%, then -13.4%.

## 5.6 Effect of measurement precision

Scenarios 8 and 10 to 12 hold the design and the true values fixed and vary
only the control coefficient of variation, from 9.6% down to 1.9%. This is the
most informative comparison in the study, because it distinguishes the two
things that can make an estimate wrong. Estimation error shrinks as an
experiment becomes more precise. Model misspecification does not.

