# CV as an ordered factor rather than a numeric axis: the four levels are not
# evenly spaced on either a linear or a log scale, and what matters is their
# order, not their spacing.
precision$cv_f <- factor(precision$cv, levels = c(9.6, 6.7, 2.8, 1.9))
```

```{r precision-bias, fig.width = 9, fig.height = 4.8, out.width = "100%", fig.cap = "**Figure 3.** Relative bias in the estimated ErC10 and ErC50 against the control coefficient of variation, which decreases to the right as the experiment becomes more precise. Each point summarises 500 simulated datasets. Approaches that retain the measurement converge on zero bias; approaches that impose the zero boundary converge on a non-zero value."}
ggplot(precision, aes(x = cv_f, y = 100 * rel_bias, colour = arm, group = arm)) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0, linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey40") +
  geom_line(linewidth = 0.7) +
  geom_point(size = 1.8) +
  # One label per series at the right-hand edge rather than a key: eight hues in
  # a legend are hard to tell apart, and each panel holds only two or three of
  # them, so the label can sit on the line it names.
  geom_text(data = subset(precision, cv_f == "1.9"), aes(label = arm),
            hjust = -0.45, size = 2.5, show.legend = FALSE) +
  scale_x_discrete(expand = expansion(add = c(0.35, 0.9))) +

  facet_grid(estimate ~ handling, scales = "free_y") +
  labs(x = "Control coefficient of variation (%)  —  more precise →",
       y = "Relative bias in the estimated ErCx (%)",
       colour = NULL) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = arm_cols, guide = "none") +
  theme_classic(base_size = 9) +
  theme(strip.background = element_blank(), legend.position = "none")
```

Arms A, C and D converge on the true value: arm A's ErC10 bias falls from
+19.1% to +1.5% and arm C's from +10.4% to +1.0%, and both ErC50 biases sit
within one per cent throughout. Arms B1, B2 and B3 level off instead, at +13.1%
and +49.5% on ErC10 for B1 and B2, and at -8.2% and -11.4% on ErC50.

Arms E and F end worse than either group. E's ErC50 bias *grows* as the
experiment improves, from -10.6% to -14.8%, and F's from -5.6% to -15.6% — the
two largest ErC50 biases in the study at the highest precision tested. Nothing
about the analysis looks wrong while this happens: E is the best-behaved
approach in the set by sampler diagnostics, producing no divergent transitions
in any of its 6,000 fits.

This also resolves the ErC10 result. At `R` = 2.3 every approach is biased high
on ErC10, by between 13% and 56%, because a 10% decline from the control has to
be resolved against a 9.6% control coefficient of variation. Once the
measurement is precise enough, the ErC10 ordering is the same as the ErC50
ordering.

## 5.7 Credible interval coverage

Bias understates the problem, because the intervals narrow as precision
improves while their centre stays displaced.

```{r precision-coverage, fig.width = 9, fig.height = 4.8, out.width = "100%", fig.cap = "**Figure 4.** Proportion of 95% credible intervals containing the true value, against the control coefficient of variation. The dashed line is nominal coverage. Under B1 and B3 the intervals shrink around a displaced centre and coverage falls to zero; under B2 they are so wide that coverage reaches one while the point estimate is 11% low."}
ggplot(precision, aes(x = cv_f, y = coverage, colour = arm, group = arm)) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.95, linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey40") +
  geom_line(linewidth = 0.7) +
  geom_point(size = 1.8) +
  # One label per series at the right-hand edge rather than a key: eight hues in
  # a legend are hard to tell apart, and each panel holds only two or three of
  # them, so the label can sit on the line it names.
  geom_text(data = subset(precision, cv_f == "1.9"), aes(label = arm),
            hjust = -0.45, size = 2.5, show.legend = FALSE) +
  scale_x_discrete(expand = expansion(add = c(0.35, 0.9))) +

  scale_y_continuous(limits = c(0, 1)) +
  facet_grid(estimate ~ handling) +
  labs(x = "Control coefficient of variation (%)  —  more precise →",
       y = "Proportion of 95% intervals containing the truth",
       colour = NULL) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = arm_cols, guide = "none") +
  theme_classic(base_size = 9) +
  theme(strip.background = element_blank(), legend.position = "none")
```

At the highest precision tested, B3 and E contained the true ErC50 in **none**
of the 500 simulated datasets and B1 in one of them, and B3 and E likewise miss
almost every ErC10 interval (0.000 and 0.126). F is not far behind at 0.116 on
ErC50. A laboratory that improves its technique, adds replicates and reduces
its control variability will, if it floors its negative values, produce an
analysis that is more confident and no less biased.

B2 fails in the opposite direction. Its coverage reaches 1.000, but its RMSE is
0.63 against arm A's 0.38 and its point estimate is 11% low: the intervals are
wide enough to contain almost anything. **Coverage should never be reported
without a measure of interval width**, or the two worst-performing approaches
here would be scored as the two best. Section 5.10 shows that B2's behaviour is
substantially a property of the single residual scale rather than of the
constraint.

## 5.8 NSEC

ErC10 and the NSEC are the two estimates commonly used as a no-effect value, and
a reader choosing between them needs to know that the conventions do not affect
them in the same way as they affect ErC50.

Its expected value at any finite level of noise is larger than the true `nec`,
for the reason given in Section 2.3, so comparing an estimated NSEC with the
true `nec` in a single scenario measures the metric's definition and the
approach's error at the same time. The precision sweep separates them: the true
`nec` is the NSEC's limiting value as the residual error goes to zero, so a
correctly specified approach must converge on it. Checked directly at
negligible noise, `nsec()` returns 1.2985 against a true `nec` of 1.3.

```{r, echo = FALSE}
nsec_precision <- read.csv(text = "
arm,cv,nsec,ratio_to_A
A,9.6,2.182,1.000
A,6.7,1.907,1.000
A,2.8,1.532,1.000
A,1.9,1.434,1.000
C,9.6,1.909,0.882
C,6.7,1.739,0.915
C,2.8,1.501,0.982
C,1.9,1.423,0.993
D,9.6,1.975,0.911
D,6.7,1.768,0.929
D,2.8,1.502,0.983
D,1.9,1.423,0.995
B2,9.6,3.195,1.462
B2,6.7,3.102,1.630
B2,2.8,3.044,1.998
B2,1.9,3.036,2.125
B3,9.6,2.225,1.014
B3,6.7,2.155,1.119
B3,2.8,2.012,1.313
B3,1.9,1.983,1.389
B1,9.6,2.107,0.959
B1,6.7,2.022,1.053
B1,2.8,1.921,1.260
B1,1.9,1.909,1.337
E,9.6,2.047,0.945
E,6.7,2.038,1.064
E,2.8,1.977,1.295
E,1.9,1.952,1.367
F,9.6,3.270,1.426
F,6.7,3.014,1.514
F,2.8,2.622,1.682
F,1.9,2.435,1.687
")
nsec_precision$handling <- factor(arm_handling[nsec_precision$arm],
                                  levels = handling_levels)
nsec_precision$arm <- factor(nsec_precision$arm, levels = arm_levels)
nsec_precision$cv_f <- factor(nsec_precision$cv, levels = c(9.6, 6.7, 2.8, 1.9))
```

```{r nsec-precision, fig.width = 9, fig.height = 4, out.width = "100%", fig.cap = "**Figure 5.** Mean estimated NSEC against the control coefficient of variation, for scenarios 8 and 10 to 12, in which the design and the true values are identical and only the measurement precision changes. The horizontal line is the true nec of 1.3, which is the value the NSEC must converge on as the residual error goes to zero."}
ggplot(nsec_precision, aes(x = cv_f, y = nsec, colour = arm, group = arm)) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 1.3, linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey30") +
  # One label per series at the right-hand edge rather than a key: eight hues in
  # a legend are hard to tell apart, and each panel holds only two or three of
  # them, so the label can sit on the line it names.
  geom_text(data = subset(nsec_precision, cv_f == "1.9"), aes(label = arm),
            hjust = -0.45, size = 2.5, show.legend = FALSE) +
  scale_x_discrete(expand = expansion(add = c(0.35, 0.9))) +

  # the reference line is identified in the caption rather than annotated on the
  # panel: the x scale is discrete here, and annotate() with a numeric position
  # errors under ggplot2 4.x
  geom_line(linewidth = 0.7) +
  geom_point(size = 1.8) +
  facet_wrap(~ handling) +
  labs(x = "Control coefficient of variation (%)  —  more precise →",
       y = "Estimated NSEC (concentration units)",
       colour = NULL) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = arm_cols, guide = "none") +
  theme_classic(base_size = 9) +
  theme(strip.background = element_blank())
```

Arms A, C and D converge cleanly, falling from 2.18, 1.91 and 1.98 to 1.43,
1.42 and 1.42 as the control coefficient of variation drops from 9.6% to 1.9%,
all heading toward 1.3. Arms B1, B3 and B2 level off well above it, at 1.91,
1.98 and 3.04, corresponding to overestimates of the true `nec` by 47%, 53% and
134%. Arms E and F do the same, at 1.95 and 2.44, overestimating by 50% and
87%.

**The two no-effect estimates are biased in the same direction, and it is the
unprotective one.** At the highest precision tested the boundary-imposing
approaches overestimate ErC10 by 13% to 50% and overestimate the true `nec` by
47% to 134%. Both make the substance appear less toxic than it is. ErC50 moves
the other way, low by 5 to 16%, which makes it appear more toxic. Anyone
treating ErC10 and the NSEC as interchangeable no-effect values should note that
flooring inflates both, and inflates the NSEC by more.

## 5.9 The model-averaged workflow

Everything above holds the equation fixed. That is the right design for
attributing a change to the treatment of the data, but it is not what an
analyst does, and on altered data it fits an equation that did not generate
them. This subsection and the next report the same simulated datasets under the
two practices an analyst would actually apply: fitting a candidate set and
averaging over it, which is what `bayesnec` recommends, and modelling the
residual scale.

We re-ran five approaches — A, C, B3, E and F — on three of the twelve
scenarios, 200 simulated datasets each, averaging every approach over the
declining candidate set. The datasets are the same ones the single-model
results used, so every comparison is paired. Models that failed to converge
(R-hat > 1.01) were dropped and the weights re-stacked over the survivors; no
approach in any scenario ever lost its whole candidate set.

We ran this sweep over the eight-equation Gaussian candidate set that
`bayesnec` retained before version 2.2.0. That set is now fourteen equations,
and the six added are the zero-asymptote shapes a floored dataset most
resembles. The direction of that change on these results has not been measured
and is not guessed at here; it is recorded in Section 8.

```{r, echo = FALSE}
# Phase 10, transcribed from analysis/phase10_vignette_numbers.txt in the
# compendium at 0181d66e, produced by analysis/phase10_vignette_numbers.R over
# analysis/phase10_metrics.csv. Figures are at n = 200 per cell: 3 cells x 200 x
# 5 arms, 27,600 model fits, 0 failures, 0 exclusions.
# bias_s is the single-model result recomputed over these same 200 datasets, not
# the 500-dataset figure quoted earlier, so the two differ slightly; the paired
# 200 is the right baseline for a paired contrast.
# Regenerate in the compendium after any change to the sweep and move the pinned
# commit in Section 9 to match. Never edit a result in place.
phase10 <- read.csv(text = "
