        panel.spacing = unit(0.5, "lines"))
```

```{r averaging-coverage, echo = FALSE, fig.width = 9, fig.height = 6.5, out.width = "100%", fig.cap = "**Figure 7.** Proportion of 95% credible intervals containing the true value, under a fixed equation and under model averaging, for the same fits as Figure 6. Each segment runs from the fixed-equation result to the averaged result, with the arrow head at the averaged value; the dashed line is nominal coverage. Averaging restores coverage everywhere except the NSEC at the highest precision, where it moves B3 from 0.00 to 0.01."}
ggplot(phase10, aes(y = arm, colour = arm)) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 0.95, linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey40") +
  geom_segment(aes(x = cov_s, xend = cov_a, yend = arm),
               arrow = arrow(length = unit(0.08, "inches"), type = "closed"),
               linewidth = 0.7) +
  geom_point(aes(x = cov_s), size = 1.7) +
  facet_grid(estimate ~ cell) +
  scale_x_continuous(limits = c(0, 1)) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = arm_cols, guide = "none") +
  labs(x = "Proportion of 95% intervals containing the truth", y = "Approach") +
  theme_classic(base_size = 9) +
  theme(strip.background = element_blank(),
        panel.spacing = unit(0.5, "lines"))
```

Averaging reduces the ErC50 bias in every scenario tested. B3 improves from
-10.2% to -3.0% at the highest precision and from -7.6% to -3.3% at a 9.6%
control CV; E from -14.8% to -5.5% and from -10.3% to -5.0%; F from -15.7% to
+0.9% and from -5.3% to +1.5%. Coverage recovers with it, B3 from 0.00 to 0.58
at the highest precision, and RMSE falls at the same time, from 0.52 to 0.17.
Intervals widen by only 1.1 to 1.6 times, so this is a reduction in error
rather than coverage obtained by widening the intervals.

The residual bias after averaging is what qualifies "reduced". On ErC50 the
worst remaining figure across the three cells is E at -5.5%, against arm A's
-0.2% to +1.2%; B3 settles at -3.0% to -3.3% and F between +0.9% and +2.4%. So
averaging removes most but not all of the ErC50 penalty, and the approach that
retains the measurement is still the least biased.

**The NSEC divides on measurement precision, and the division is the reason for
Figures 6 and 7.** At the noise levels of a real test, averaging repairs it: B3's bias
changes from +72.9% to -1.7% at a 9.6% control CV and from +41.4% to -6.0% at
`delta` = 8. At the highest precision tested it does the opposite, B3 changing
from +52.7% to -62.7%, with coverage falling from 0.00 to 0.01 and RMSE
worsening from 0.69 to 0.82. E and F reverse in the same way, to -42.1% and
-53.5%.

The reversal is consistent with which equations take the weight. The floored
approaches load their weight onto `ecxwb2` and `ecxwb1`, which decay smoothly
from the control with no breakpoint, so the averaged curve leaves the control
immediately. At high precision the control posterior is tight, the NSEC
reference sits just below the control mean, and a curve that departs
immediately crosses that reference very early, which drives the estimate below
the true `nec`. At realistic noise the reference sits far below the control,
the crossing is further to the right, and the difference in curve shape matters
much less. This account is consistent with the sign of the reversal and with
the fitted weights, but it has not been tested directly.

Two further results qualify what averaging achieves. Arm F's NSEC remains
+82.2% at a 9.6% control CV after averaging, against arm A's +16.0%, so
flooring through a Gamma leaves a large residual effect under the recommended
workflow. And averaging increases the ErC10 bias: F changes from +15.7% to
-23.7% at the highest precision with coverage falling from 0.98 to 0.50, and
even arm A changes from +19.0% to -11.3% at a 9.6% control CV. Averaging over
shapes that disagree about the shoulder is not an obvious way to resolve a 10%
decline against the residual scatter, so these approaches should not be
summarised across toxicity estimates.

## 5.10 A dispersion sub-model

Every fit above holds `sigma` constant along the curve while the data
were generated with a residual scale varying 8.1-fold. That misspecification is
shared by all eight approaches, including arm A, whose estimates are
nonetheless unbiased; what it affects is the interval, and it affects B2 most.
`bnec()` provides a dispersion sub-model, and for a response that crosses zero
the available form is `disp("loglinear")`; `disp("power")` is refused for
exactly this case, because a power law in the mean is undefined where the mean
crosses zero. See the *Non-constant dispersion* section of the
[Single model usage][e1] vignette.

We checked this on scenario 8, fitting the six Gaussian approaches twice each to
50 datasets, once homoscedastic and once under `disp("loglinear")`, paired so
that every contrast is within-dataset. The result splits the approaches in two.

The case against flooring is unaffected. B1's ErC50 bias changes from -9.0% to
-8.5% and B3's from -10.7% to -9.8%, with coverage still 0.50 and 0.44.
Flooring damages the mean structure, and modelling the residual scale does not
repair that.

B2's poor performance, by contrast, is a property of the residual model rather
than of the constraint. Its ErC50 bias changes from -12.7% to -1.2%, its NSEC
overestimate from 1.49 times arm A to 1.06, and its divergent transitions from
3.22 per fit to 0.12. All three symptoms have one cause: a single `sigma` asked
to cover both the control scatter and observations the constrained curve cannot
reach. Given a dispersion sub-model, fixing the asymptote at zero on data that
were never floored is close to unbiased. The check is recorded in
`notes/disp_sensitivity_example7.md`.

# 6 Worked case studies

The simulation establishes which approaches are right and which give relatively
unbiased estimates. It cannot show how large the difference is on a real
dataset, where the truth is unknown and the design, the noise and the shape of
the response are not under the analyst's control. We therefore analysed four
marine microalgal growth tests with the six Gaussian approaches, and compared
each estimate with arm A's on the same dataset.

The four tests ship with `bayesnec` as `alga`, so everything in this section
is computed when the vignette is built rather than transcribed: two tests on
*Cladocopium proliferum*, a coral endosymbiont, over seven days, and two on
*Rhodomonas salina* over three days, each species exposed to two contaminants
on undisclosed and mutually incomparable concentration scales. We do not apply
arms E and F here: on a real dataset there is no true value to score them
against, and what they add to the study is that their bias survives to the
noise-free limit, which can only be shown where the truth is known.

```{r}
data(alga)

# The four case studies are the four species-by-contaminant cells of `alga`.
# The names used throughout this section are the vignette's; the mapping is by
# cell rather than by row order, so it survives any reordering of the data.
alga$dataset <- factor(
  paste0(as.character(alga$species), ifelse(alga$contaminant == "B", "2", "")),
  levels = c("c_proliferum", "c_proliferum2", "r_salina", "r_salina2"))

# The growth rate implied by the counting limit of 10 cells per mL, against the
# starting density recorded for the test. Where the source substituted a zero
# for a below-limit count -- flagged `sgr_source == "substituted"` -- this is the
# rate the substitution implies, and it is what the intact analysis uses in
# place of the substituted zero.
alga$bound <- (log(10) - log(alga$density_initial)) / alga$days
alga$below_limit <- alga$sgr_source == "substituted"

datasets <- alga |>
  group_by(dataset, species, duration_d = days) |>
  summarise(n = n(), n_conc = n_distinct(dose),
            control_rate = mean(sgr[dose == 0]),
            below_limit = sum(below_limit),
            prop_at_or_below_zero = mean(sgr <= 0), .groups = "drop") |>
  mutate(fold_change_R = exp(control_rate * duration_d), .before = below_limit)
```

```{r, echo = FALSE}
# Transposed: nine columns against four rows reads badly, and the datasets are
# the natural columns because every later figure compares across them. Each
# value is formatted before transposing -- t() coerces to character, so kable's
# `digits` no longer applies once the table is on its side.
datasets_t <- with(datasets, data.frame(
  Species = as.character(species),
  Days = as.character(duration_d),
  n = as.character(n),
  Concentrations = as.character(n_conc),
  `Control rate (per d)` = sprintf("%.3f", control_rate),
  `Fold change R` = sprintf("%.1f", fold_change_R),
  `Below limit` = as.character(below_limit),
  `Prop. at or below zero` = sprintf("%.2f", prop_at_or_below_zero),
  check.names = FALSE, stringsAsFactors = FALSE))
datasets_t <- as.data.frame(t(datasets_t), stringsAsFactors = FALSE)
names(datasets_t) <- datasets$dataset
knitr::kable(datasets_t,
  caption = "**Table 4.** The four case-study datasets, computed from `alga`. All are in the regime where the design reaches the zero-growth boundary. `Below limit` counts the rows whose population fell below the counting limit, for which the source substituted a zero.")
```

## 6.1 Undefined growth rates in the *Rhodomonas* tests

At the highest concentrations in both *Rhodomonas* tests the population fell
below the counting limit of 10 cells per mL, so the density is recorded as zero
and the specific growth rate is *undefined* rather than negative. Those rows
are flagged `sgr_source == "substituted"` in `alga`, 16 on `r_salina` and 4 on
`r_salina2`. The two *Cladocopium* tests have none, and their negative values
are measurements.

There is no measured value to retain in those rows, so every approach must
substitute something. The intact analysis uses the growth rate implied by the
counting limit, which is itself a substitution, and on those two datasets no
approach is therefore a true intact analysis. The comparison is correspondingly
weaker there. This is a common situation with real algal data and one of the
reasons left-censoring is useful: it can express "at or below the limit"
without inventing a value.

It also determines what arm C means on those two datasets. Left-censoring
declares that the truth lies at or below a bound, so the bound should be the
tightest one the measurement supports: zero for a growth rate that was measured
and came out negative, but the counting limit itself for a row that fell below
it, which is a stronger statement than "below zero" and is the one thing the
test does establish about that row. Censoring such a row at zero instead
changes the ErC50 by 3 to 6% and the NSEC by 26 to 30%, in both cases away from
the value the retained-measurement arms give.

## 6.2 Estimates from each approach

```{r, results = "hide"}
# One dataset's six arms. The prior is pulled from A and passed to the other
# five for the reason given in Section 5.1: bnec() derives the default Gaussian
# priors from the response vector, and on r_salina2 the substituted zeros pull
# the default bot prior onto zero, so letting each arm take its own default
# would leak the convention under test into the prior.
fit_arms <- function(d) {
  # The intact analysis restores the counting-limit rate where the source
  # substituted a zero. On the two Cladocopium tests nothing is substituted and
  # this is the measured series unchanged.
  d$y <- ifelse(d$below_limit, d$bound, d$sgr)

  fit_A <- bnec(y ~ crf(dose, "nec4param"), data = d,
                family = gaussian(link = "identity"), seed = 333,
                open_progress = FALSE)
  shared <- get_priors(fit_A)
  pinned <- shared
  pinned$prior[pinned$nlpar == "bot"] <- "constant(0)"

  d_floored <- transform(d, y = pmax(y, 0))
  # Left-censoring declares only that the truth lies at or below a bound, so the
  # bound should be the tightest one the measurement supports. For a measured
  # negative that is zero -- withdrawing the magnitude is the convention under
  # test. For a row whose population fell below the counting limit it is the
  # limit itself, which is a stronger statement than "below zero" and is what is
  # actually known; censoring such a row at zero would discard the one thing the
  # test does tell you about it. See the Limits of the censored likelihood
  # section of the Single model usage vignette.
  d_cens <- transform(d,
    cens = ifelse(below_limit | y < 0, "left", "none"),
    y = ifelse(below_limit, bound, pmax(y, 0)))
  # D's cut-off is the lowest tested concentration at which A's own posterior
  # mean has reached zero, which is how the approach is defined -- it cannot be
  # applied without first fitting A.
  mu_A <- fitted(fit_A, newdata = data.frame(dose = d$dose))[, "Estimate"]
  cross <- suppressWarnings(min(d$dose[mu_A <= 0]))

  list(
    A  = fit_A,
    C  = bnec(y | cens(cens) ~ crf(dose, "nec4param"), data = d_cens,
              family = gaussian(link = "identity"), prior = shared,
              seed = 333, open_progress = FALSE),
    D  = bnec(y ~ crf(dose, "nec4param"), data = subset(d, dose < cross),
              family = gaussian(link = "identity"), prior = shared,
              seed = 333, open_progress = FALSE),
    B1 = bnec(y ~ crf(dose, "nec4param"), data = d_floored,
              family = gaussian(link = "identity"), prior = shared,
              seed = 333, open_progress = FALSE),
    B2 = bnec(y ~ crf(dose, "nec4param"), data = d,
              family = gaussian(link = "identity"), prior = pinned,
              seed = 333, open_progress = FALSE),
    B3 = bnec(y ~ crf(dose, "nec4param"), data = d_floored,
              family = gaussian(link = "identity"), prior = pinned,
              seed = 333, open_progress = FALSE))
}

real_fits <- lapply(split(alga, alga$dataset), fit_arms)
```

```{r}
# ErC50 and NSEC from every fit, with the tested concentration range recorded
# alongside so that an interval running to the edge of the design can be
# flagged rather than read as an estimate.
real_estimates <- do.call(rbind, lapply(names(real_fits), function(ds) {
  rng <- range(alga$dose[alga$dataset == ds & alga$dose > 0])
  do.call(rbind, lapply(names(real_fits[[ds]]), function(a) {
    f <- real_fits[[ds]][[a]]
    e <- ecx(f, ecx_val = 50, type = "absolute")
    n <- nsec(f)
    rbind(data.frame(dataset = ds, arm = a, estimate = "ErC50",
                     val = e[1], lo = e[2], hi = e[3]),
          data.frame(dataset = ds, arm = a, estimate = "NSEC",
                     val = n[1], lo = n[2], hi = n[3])) |>
      transform(x_min = rng[1], x_max = rng[2])
  }))
}))

# Expressed as a ratio to the intact analysis on the same dataset, because the
# four tests are on undisclosed and mutually incomparable concentration scales.
# An estimate is flagged unusable where its interval has collapsed to a point --
# truncation can leave too few concentrations above the crossing to support one
# -- or has run to an edge of the tested range, where the bound is the design
# rather than the data.
real_ratios <- real_estimates |>
  group_by(dataset, estimate) |>
  mutate(ref = val[arm == "A"],
         r = val / ref, rlo = lo / ref, rhi = hi / ref,
         usable = !(lo <= x_min | hi >= x_max | hi - lo < .Machine$double.eps^0.5)) |>
  ungroup() |>
  select(dataset, arm, estimate, r, rlo, rhi, usable)
```

```{r, echo = FALSE}
# Every figure the prose below quotes is computed here rather than typed, so a
# refit cannot leave the text describing an earlier one.
rr <- function(ds, a, est = "ErC50", what = "r") {
  real_ratios[[what]][real_ratios$dataset == ds & real_ratios$arm == a &
                        real_ratios$estimate == est]
}
wid <- function(ds, a, est = "ErC50") (rr(ds, a, est, "rhi") - rr(ds, a, est, "rlo")) /
  (rr(ds, "A", est, "rhi") - rr(ds, "A", est, "rlo"))
fl <- c("B1", "B3")                      # the two floored arms
# How many of the four datasets an arm was flagged unusable on, computed rather
# than stated: which arms are flagged depends on the fits, and a count typed
# into the prose goes stale silently the next time the vignette is precompiled.
n_unusable <- function(a, est = "ErC50") {
  sum(!real_ratios$usable[real_ratios$arm == a & real_ratios$estimate == est])
}
f1 <- function(x) sprintf("%.1f", x)
# a width ratio can be two figures or three; a trailing ".0" on 116 reads oddly
fw <- function(x) if (x >= 10) sprintf("%.0f", x) else sprintf("%.1f", x)
f2 <- function(x) sprintf("%.2f", x)
pc <- function(x) sprintf("%.0f%%", 100 * x)
```

Because the four datasets have toxicity values on completely different
concentration scales, each estimate is shown as a ratio: the approach's
estimate divided by arm A's on the same dataset. A ratio of 1 is exact
agreement with the intact analysis.

```{r real-ratios, fig.width = 9, fig.height = 6, out.width = "100%", fig.cap = "**Figure 8.** Estimated ErC50 and NSEC from each approach on the four real datasets, expressed as a ratio to the intact analysis (A) on the same dataset, with 95% credible intervals. The vertical line at 1 is exact agreement with the intact analysis. Open symbols mark estimates flagged as unusable, meaning the interval reached an edge of the tested concentration range, where the bound is the design rather than the data, or collapsed to a point. Intervals extending past the plotted range are clipped at the panel edge."}
real_ratios$arm <- factor(real_ratios$arm, levels = rev(arm_levels))

# B2's lower bound runs to the bottom of the tested range on two datasets, which
# on a log axis is four decades and compresses every other estimate onto the
# reference line. Those estimates are already flagged unusable, so the bar is
# clipped to the panel and the open symbol marks it. Clipping by
# pmax/pmin on the data rather than a scale limit, to avoid depending on
# `scales` for an oob handler.
ratio_lim <- c(0.1, 4)
real_ratios$rlo_p <- pmax(real_ratios$rlo, ratio_lim[1])
real_ratios$rhi_p <- pmin(real_ratios$rhi, ratio_lim[2])

ggplot(real_ratios, aes(x = r, y = arm, colour = arm, shape = usable)) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 1, linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey40") +
  geom_errorbar(aes(xmin = rlo_p, xmax = rhi_p), orientation = "y",
                width = 0, linewidth = 0.6) +
  geom_point(size = 2.2, fill = "white") +
  scale_shape_manual(values = c("TRUE" = 16, "FALSE" = 21), guide = "none") +
  scale_x_log10(breaks = c(0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, 4), limits = ratio_lim) +
  facet_grid(estimate ~ dataset) +
  labs(x = "Estimate as a ratio to the intact analysis (log scale)",
       y = "Approach") +
  scale_colour_manual(values = arm_cols) +
  theme_classic(base_size = 9) +
  theme(strip.background = element_blank(), legend.position = "none")
```

Three features of Figure 8 require care in what they do and do not establish.

The choice of approach changes the reported value substantially. On
`c_proliferum` the floored analyses return an ErC50
`r f2(min(sapply(fl, function(a) rr("c_proliferum", a))))` to
`r f2(max(sapply(fl, function(a) rr("c_proliferum", a))))` times arm A's, and
B2 returns `r f2(rr("c_proliferum", "B2"))` times it. On `r_salina` B2 returns
`r f2(rr("r_salina", "B2"))` times arm A's ErC50 and
`r f2(rr("r_salina", "B2", "NSEC"))` times its NSEC. These are not rounding
differences; they are the difference between one regulatory value and another.

The size of that difference varies between datasets and cannot be predicted
from the data alone. On `c_proliferum2` the floored analyses are within
`r pc(max(abs(sapply(fl, function(a) rr("c_proliferum2", a)) - 1)))` of arm A.
The simulation is unambiguous about the direction of the flooring bias, but a
single real dataset cannot establish its size, because arm A on that dataset
has its own error and the two are not separable without a known truth.

B2's intervals are frequently unusable. Its ErC50 interval reached an edge of
the tested concentration range on `r n_unusable("B2")` of the four datasets,
which is why those estimates are shown as open symbols: there the bound is the
design rather than the data. Where it did not, its intervals are
`r fw(min(wid("c_proliferum", "B2"), wid("r_salina", "B2")))` and
`r fw(max(wid("c_proliferum", "B2"), wid("r_salina", "B2")))` times wider than
arm A's. Fixing the asymptote at zero while leaving genuinely negative data in
place asks the model to fit a curve that cannot reach its own observations, and
the sampler reports this as divergent transitions and very wide intervals.
Section 5.10 shows that a dispersion sub-model removes most of this.

The NSEC panel is consistent with the simulation on the dataset best placed to
show it. `r_salina2` has the largest proportion of observations at or below
zero of the four, and there all three boundary-imposing approaches return an
NSEC between
`r f1(min(sapply(c("B1","B2","B3"), function(a) rr("r_salina2", a, "NSEC"))))`
and
`r f1(max(sapply(c("B1","B2","B3"), function(a) rr("r_salina2", a, "NSEC"))))`
times arm A's — the same upward direction, and a similar magnitude, to the
47–134% overestimates measured in the simulation.

## 6.3 Sensitivity to the `bot` prior

Under a Gaussian likelihood with no lower plateau in the data, `bot` is
substantially prior-informed, so it is reasonable to ask whether the
differences reported above are differences in the prior rather than in the
likelihood. We checked this on `c_proliferum` by refitting each approach with a
`bot` prior four times wider and four times tighter than the default, a
sixteen-fold range of prior scale. B2 and B3 are excluded because their `bot`
is fixed.

```{r, echo = FALSE}
prior_sens <- read.csv(text = "
arm,estimate,tighter,default,wider,estimate_span_pct,bot_tighter,bot_default,bot_wider,bot_span_x
A,ErC10,1.64,1.62,1.56,4.94,-0.5035,-1.0218,-2.3929,4.75
B1,ErC10,2.72,2.78,2.76,2.16,-0.0298,-0.0243,-0.0244,1.22
C,ErC10,2.66,2.60,2.56,3.85,-0.3159,-0.6082,-1.6192,5.13
D,ErC10,2.53,2.53,2.55,0.59,-0.2700,-0.4040,-1.2484,4.62
A,ErC50,3.16,3.32,3.42,7.83,-0.5035,-1.0218,-2.3929,4.75
B1,ErC50,6.41,6.35,6.37,0.95,-0.0298,-0.0243,-0.0244,1.22
C,ErC50,6.49,6.55,6.59,1.53,-0.3159,-0.6082,-1.6192,5.13
D,ErC50,5.00,5.00,5.00,0.00,-0.2700,-0.4040,-1.2484,4.62
A,NSEC,2.00,1.96,1.94,2.99,-0.5035,-1.0218,-2.3929,4.75
B1,NSEC,2.24,2.33,2.30,3.86,-0.0298,-0.0243,-0.0244,1.22
C,NSEC,2.08,2.00,1.93,7.75,-0.3159,-0.6082,-1.6192,5.13
D,NSEC,1.50,1.50,1.51,0.82,-0.2700,-0.4040,-1.2484,4.62
")
```

```{r, echo = FALSE}
knitr::kable(
  prior_sens |>
    filter(estimate == "ErC50") |>
    select(arm, bot_tighter, bot_default, bot_wider, bot_span_x),
  digits = c(0, 3, 3, 3, 2),
  col.names = c("Approach", "bot (tighter prior)", "bot (default)",
                "bot (wider prior)", "range, as a multiple"),
  caption = "**Table 5.** Posterior mean lower asymptote on c_proliferum under three bot priors spanning a sixteen-fold range of scale."
)
```

```{r, echo = FALSE}
knitr::kable(
  prior_sens |>
    select(arm, estimate, estimate_span_pct) |>
    pivot_wider(names_from = estimate, values_from = estimate_span_pct),
  digits = 2,
  col.names = c("Approach", "ErC10", "ErC50", "NSEC"),
  caption = "**Table 6.** How far each reported toxicity estimate changes across the same sixteen-fold change in the bot prior, as a percentage of its value under the default prior."
)
```

The plateau is strongly prior-dependent in every approach that estimates it,
changing by a factor of 4.6 to 5.1, and under B1 by a factor of only 1.2 —
because it is being determined by the substituted zeros rather than by the
prior or the measurements. A parameter that is robust to its prior is normally
reassuring; here it indicates that the data have been replaced by a constant.

The reported toxicity estimates are robust in every approach. The largest
change anywhere in Table 6 is 7.8%, against a gap of about 33% between arm A
and B2 on the same dataset. The differences this vignette reports are therefore
differences in the likelihood. The toxicity estimates can be reported even
where the parameter that generates them cannot.

# 7 Recommendations

**Analyse the growth rates as measured, and let the model estimate the lower
asymptote.** Nothing in a Gaussian concentration-response fit requires the
response to be positive. In the simulation this approach is unbiased to within
one per cent on ErC50 and converges on the truth for every toxicity estimate.

**Choose the curve shape on its merits, separately from the data.** A lower
asymptote fixed at zero, representing complete inhibition, is available under a
Gaussian family from version 2.2.0 and an absolute ECx is reported for it.
Fitted on intact measurements with a dispersion sub-model it is close to
unbiased (-1.2%). Fitted on floored data it is among the worst performers in
this study. The shape is not the problem; the substitution is.

**If the negative values were never recorded, declare them as censored rather
than substituting a value.** Left-censoring recovers essentially all of what
flooring loses — an ErC50 bias of -0.3% against arm A's +0.6% — and it is the
only option when the underlying measurements are gone. Where the lower
asymptote is *entirely* below the censoring bound the likelihood is flat in
`bot` and its posterior is the prior's.

**Do not choose a zero-bounded response distribution to solve the problem.**
This is the most common response to negative growth rates. Under a fixed
equation it produces the two largest ErC50 biases in the study at the highest
precision tested, -14.8% and -15.6%. Under the model-averaged workflow the
ErC50 penalty is much reduced, but arm F's NSEC is still +82.2% at a realistic
noise level against arm A's +16.0%. Neither approach gives any sign of trouble:
across all 6,000 of its fits, E produced not one divergent transition.

**If you have floored data and cannot recover it, average over a candidate set
rather than fitting one equation.** On ErC50 the worst residual bias after
averaging is -5.5%, against -14.8% for the same approach under a fixed
equation, and coverage recovers with it. Do not expect it to repair the NSEC at
high precision, where averaging makes it worse.

**Do not convert to percent inhibition before fitting.** Expressing the
response as a percentage of the control caps it at 100%, which makes negative
growth look like an error and pushes the analyst toward a bounded family.
`ecx(type = "absolute")` already expresses the effect relative to the fitted
control, so the transformation is unnecessary as well as harmful; @Ritz2026
reach the same conclusion by a different route. `bayesnec` will warn if it
detects data that appear to have been normalised.

**Report the proportion of observations at or below zero.** This is the single
number that tells a reader whether the question arises for the dataset at all.
Below a few per cent, none of the effects described here are detectable; at 15%
or more they are the size reported above.

**Do not report interval coverage, or interval width, alone.** B2 achieves
perfect coverage with an 11%-low point estimate and enormous intervals; B1, B3
and E achieve narrow intervals with zero coverage.

**Model weights describe the data in front of them.** On floored data the
generating equation receives a thousandth of the weight, because floored data
are no longer draws from it. A weight is evidence about which shape fits the
data supplied, not about which process produced the measurements.

# 8 Limitations

**Neither arm A nor arm C reaches nominal coverage on ErC50.** In the reaching
regime arm A holds 0.89–0.95 and arm C 0.77–0.85 against a nominal 0.95. Both
are far better than the alternatives, but neither is perfectly calibrated, and
the shortfall is at least partly attributable to the next point.

**Residual error is generated heteroscedastic and fitted homoscedastic**
throughout the simulation. We ran the sweep before the dispersion interface
existed, so the coverage figures should be read as reflecting a single pooled
`sigma` standing in for a residual scale that varies 8.1-fold along the
curve. The check in Section 5.10 is one scenario at one noise level, and
`"loglinear"` is itself an approximation to the linear `sigma` ramp the data
were generated from. It shows the ordering is dispersion-dependent for B2; it
does not re-score the study. Re-running the precision sweep under
`disp("loglinear")` is tracked in issue #283.

**We ran the model-averaging sweep over an eight-equation candidate set**, as
stated in Section 5.9. Re-running it over the fourteen equations retained from
version 2.2.0 would establish whether the recovery reported there holds when
the zero-asymptote shapes are among the candidates. That is the largest
outstanding gap in this vignette.

**The two family-imposed approaches use `nec3param` and take their own default
priors.** Both differences are deliberate and both limit what their contrast
means. Running a Beta or a Gamma under `nec4param`, where the asymptote is free
but still bounded below by the family's support, is a separate question that is
not answered here.

**The case studies fit one equation.** In the simulation, fixing the model at
`nec4param` is unambiguously right, because it is the generating equation. For
the four real datasets it was fixed for comparability across approaches, and it
is not what an analyst using `bayesnec` would do. The case-study estimates
should be read as showing that the choice of convention changes the answer, not
as recommendations for how to analyse those particular tests.

**We ran the precision sweep at one combination of the other two factors**
(`delta` = 4, `top_factor` = 2.0). The convergence behaviour is expected to be
general, since it follows from the boundary being a fixed misspecification, but
it has been demonstrated at one point of the design rather than across it.

# 9 Provenance of the reported results

The simulation in Section 5 is not run when this vignette is built. It
takes days of machine time on many cores, which is far more than a package
build can use, and it depends on a `targets` pipeline and an `renv` lockfile
that are not part of `bayesnec`. It is a separate research compendium,
[open-AIMS/negative-sgr](https://github.com/open-AIMS/negative-sgr)
[@negativesgr2026], which contains the generating code, the pipeline that runs
it, the lockfile pinning every package version, and the result tables.

Every figure quoted from the simulation is transcribed from that compendium's
output at commit `0181d66e`. The tables that can be regenerated and checked
against this document are `analysis/phase5_metrics.csv` for the eight-approach
comparison and `analysis/phase10_metrics.csv` for the model-averaging results,
with `analysis/phase10_vignette_numbers.R` producing the summary quoted in
Section 5.9.

The case studies in Section 6 are computed when the vignette is built, from the
`alga` data that ship with `bayesnec`, so they track the current version of
`bayesnec` while the simulation figures describe it as it behaved at that
commit.

